\title{\textbf{Bayesian Network Templates}}
\author{Carlos López}
\date{June 2023}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\tableofcontents

\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Concepts behind Bayesian Networks}

A Bayesian network (BN) is a probabilistic graphical model that represents a set of variables and their conditional dependencies via a directed acyclic graph (DAG). The DAG is used to capture the relationships and knowledge about the interdependencies of these variables.

Bayesian networks are used to determine the joint probability distribution \(p(\mathbf{X})\) of a set of random variables \(\mathbf{X}\) by using conditional probability and applying the chain rule:

\begin{align}
p(\mathbf{X}) = p(X_1) \prod_{k=2}^{n}p(X_k|X_{k-1},....,X_1)
\end{align}

Where \(p(X_k|X_{k-1},....,X_1)\) represents the conditional probability of node \(X_k\) given its parents \(X_{k-1},....,X_1 \) in the DAG.

Each node in the network is associated with a probability distribution table, also known as its CPT or conditional probability table. The CPT provides the conditional probability values for each child variable given the combination of values for its parent variables.

One of the advantages of using Bayesian networks is that they allow the propagation of information from the variables with known values to the variables with uncertain values. This feature makes them suitable for reasoning under uncertainty and decision making in complex systems.

In this book, we will discussing various examples and techniques for constructing Bayesian networks and performing probabilistic inference with them.

\chapter{Examples of Bayesian Networks}

In this chapter, we will explore some examples of Bayesian networks to illustrate their structure, construction process, and inference methods.

\section{The Seafood Poisoning Problem}

Imagine a seafood restaurant, where customers can choose between fish, oysters, or clams for their dinner. Suppose that there are three types of seafood-related illnesses: fish poisoning, oyster poisoning, and clam poisoning. Customers may also have allergies to different seafood types. Our goal is to determine the probability of a customer having seafood poisoning or an allergy based on their meal choice.

\subsection{Constructing the Bayesian Network}

We can represent this problem as a Bayesian network with the following variables:

* \(F\): Fish
* \(O\): Oysters
* \(C\): Clams
* \(P_{fish}\): Fish poisoning
* \(P_{oyster}\): Oyster poisoning
* \(P_{clam}\): Clam poisoning
* \(I_{fish}\): Fish allergy
* \(I_{oyster}\): Oyster allergy
* \(I_{clam}\): Clam allergy

We'll define the conditional probability tables (CPTs) for our Bayesian network, using the following prior knowledge:

* The prevalence of seafood poisoning types is as follows:
	+ \(P(P_{fish}) = 0.05\)
	+ \(P(P_{oyster}) = 0.02\)
	+ \(P(P_{clam}) = 0.01\)
* The probability of having a seafood allergy is \(P(I_x) = 0.05\), for all \(x \in \{F, O, C\}\)
* The probabilities of having a specific allergy given a specific meal choice is as follows:
	+ \(P(I_F|F) = 0.10\)
	+ \(P(I_O|O) = 0.15\)
	+ \(P(I_C|C) = 0.10\)
* The conditional probabilities of having seafood poisoning given a specific meal choice are as follows:
	+ \(P(P_{fish}|F) = 0.30\)
	+ \(P(P_{oyster}|O) = 0.15\)
	+ \(P(P_{clam}|C) = 0.05\)

Using this information, we can draw the Bayesian network for our problem. Each node represents a variable, and the arrows indicate the dependencies between the variables. Figure \ref{fig:seafoodBN} shows the seafood poisoning problem as a Bayesian network.

\begin{figure}[h]
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=3 cm, auto, every node/.style ={font=\sffamily\scriptsize}]

\node [obs] (F) {F};
\node [obs, right of=F] (O) {O};
\node [obs, right of=O] (C) {C};
\node [hid, below left of=F, yshift=-1 cm] (P_fish) {P_{fish}};
\node [hid, below left of=O, yshift=-1 cm] (P_oyster) {P_{oyster}};
\node [hid, below left of=C, yshift=-1 cm] (P_clam) {P_{clam}};
\node [hid, right of=F, xshift=1.5 cm] (I_fish) {I_{fish}};
\node [hid, right of=O, xshift=1.5 cm] (I_oyster) {I_{oyster}};
\node [hid, right of=C, xshift=1.5 cm] (I_clam) {I_{clam}};

\path
(F) edge (P_fish)
(O) edge (P_oyster)
(C) edge (P_clam)
(F) edge (I_fish)
(O) edge (I_oyster)
(C) edge (I_clam);

\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
\caption{Bayesian network for the seafood poisoning problem.}
\label{fig:seafoodBN}
\end{figure}

